their questions for you could have answered them all during a commercial break you just shut up

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:59.100
For one minute, I am 24 years old.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:06.180
I have two GCEs, 13 years of schooling and three terms at an adult education centre behind me, right?

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:10.240
And with all that, what have I become? I'm a lookout.

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:16.590
No, Rodney, you're wrong. You're not just a lookout.

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:18.480
You're a bad lookout!

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:22.230
Alright, alright, so I'm not very good at it.

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:24.850
Perhaps that's because my art's not really in it.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:27.410
I'm not asking you to put your art in it, just your eyes will do!

00:37:28.730 --> 00:37:29.970
Yeah, well, I'm trying to...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.980
I was going to say, thinking of breaking up the partnership.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:42.700
Partnership?

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:44.620
Who's?

00:37:45.040 --> 00:37:49.660
What do you want to do that for? We're doing well. Business is booming. Profits are up. What more do you want?

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:56.800
I want to make my own decisions. And I've made one, Del. I'm going it alone.

00:37:56.960 --> 00:37:57.380
Who with?

00:37:57.520 --> 00:37:58.220
Mickey Pearce.

00:37:58.220 --> 00:37:59.260
Mickey Pearce!

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.130
Pierce? Oh, leave it out.

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.030
He couldn't keep a rabbit going with lettuce.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:07.850
You want to watch show young Piercey is a bit too fly for my liking?

00:38:08.110 --> 00:38:10.430
He'd drop his own grandmother, he would.

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:12.670
Oh, don't be stupid, Grandad.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:14.240
That was never proved.

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.380
Anyway, you give credit where it's due, right?

00:38:17.910 --> 00:38:19.930
Mickey's quite an astute businessman

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:22.350
and he's putting capital into this venture.

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:24.710
Oh, he's putting money in, is he?

00:38:25.900 --> 00:38:28.340
Well, no, but he will as soon as his gyro check arrives.

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:31.990
I see. What are you putting in?

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:33.800
I've got money, Dale.

00:38:34.100 --> 00:38:34.620
Oh, have you?

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:37.330
Yeah. I've got my offer to partnership.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:39.800
What partnership? What our pa-

00:38:49.110 --> 00:38:51.530
Right, if that is the way that you want it, my son.

00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:55.200
You're going to have to understand one thing, Rodney.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:58.200
Going it alone means exactly what it says.

00:38:58.880 --> 00:38:59.980
Right, from now on, you've got to-

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.020
I'll pay your own way in the world.

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:03.760
You pay your own way in the pubs and you pay your own way in this house.

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:07.100
You make a mistake, you stick by that mistake, all right?

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:07.560
Fine.

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:08.040
Fine.

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:10.250
And if things don't go right for you,

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.270
I don't want to hear no moaning or whining from you.

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:14.290
Look, I want moaning or whining about nothing.

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:16.480
Right. There you go, then.

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:25.220
Oh, dear Lord, in me as a work, can you give me these?

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:28.720
Yeah, well, I mean, you know, business is a bit shaky, profits are down.

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:29.980
Hold on a minute, just...

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:31.200
You said we were doing well?

00:39:31.630 --> 00:39:34.750
Yeah, well, we are doing well, relatively speaking, Rodney.

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:38.970
I mean, we are doing well compared to an Iranian gin salesman.

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:43.710
Anyway, I had to buy some stock off Alfie Flowers yesterday,

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:46.030
and, I mean, a trader is only as good as his stock, right?

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:49.560
All right. Well, this'll have to do, then, won't it?

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:53.540
But I'm going to prove to you that I have got business acumen,

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:55.410
that I'm as quick-witted as you, Del.

00:39:56.820 --> 00:39:58.320
I'll see you down the auction tomorrow.

00:39:58.630 --> 00:39:59.980
All right. How will I reckon...

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:02.640
I'll wear that stripy tie with...

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:11.360
Lot 35, ladies and gentlemen, is a consignment of smoke-damaged fire alarm.

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:17.160
Now, they're industrial models, all guaranteed to be in perfect working order.

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:21.640
Well, we've written evidence from the night watchman to say they all went off when the factory went up.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:27.000
Now, there's 70, all told, and they usually retail around the 30 quid mark.

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:29.530
So, I can start the bidding at £50 a lot.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.000
Go on, bid for them.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:34.370
What do you want industrial fire alarms for, eh?

00:40:34.870 --> 00:40:37.370
How many factories do we know are going to catch fire?

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:39.870
All right, Rodney.

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:42.380
Good morning, Derek.

00:40:43.820 --> 00:40:44.820
Hello, young Michael.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:45.820
What you do?

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:47.170
It's good here, isn't it?

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:49.940
Terrific.

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:52.200
This is my first auction.

00:40:53.010 --> 00:40:54.050
I thought it might be.

00:40:55.470 --> 00:40:56.830
Listen, a word of advice.

00:40:57.670 --> 00:40:59.910
You've got to be very careful what you do with your hands.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:00.920
in a place like this.

00:41:01.040 --> 00:41:03.420
I mean, I know you didn't realise it, Mickey,

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:05.780
but just now you put in a 40 quid bid

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:08.740
for an electric generator when you scratched your bum.

00:41:09.100 --> 00:41:09.900
Do you know what?

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:11.950
He's winding you up.

00:41:13.730 --> 00:41:14.770
Let's stay with him.

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:18.440
What are you after?

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:20.230
Glass goblets.

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:21.700
No, no, we ain't.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:23.460
But I thought you said no, no.

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:25.060
We're not after nothing in particular.

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:27.240
Oh, I see.

00:41:27.660 --> 00:41:29.970
Now listen, the one that you want to...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:36.300
beware of is lot 37 it's nothing more than a load of old scrap iron right so be careful

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:52.470
see you later 130 do i hear any more 130 down a young tower's out now ladies and gentlemen we

00:41:52.840 --> 00:41:59.980
come to lot 36 112 pieces of near-perfect cut glass goblets take a look ladies and

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.340
Gentlemen, this is us, Rodney.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:02.750
No, hang on a minute.

00:42:03.550 --> 00:42:05.430
Let's have another look at Lot 37.

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:08.170
Yeah, but Del told us to be careful with that one.

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:09.930
Yeah, and why do you think he did that?

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:12.110
Usual noddle, Mickey.

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:14.690
Del's after Lot 37, ain't he?

00:42:15.410 --> 00:42:18.450
He's just trying to put us off and leave the field open for him, ain't he?

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:20.910
I know how his mind works, son.

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:23.790
Right, Lot 37.

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:26.490
Assorted agricultural machinery.

00:42:27.490 --> 00:42:28.410
Hey, that could be anything.

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:29.960
That could be tractor...

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:31.680
Combined harvesters.

00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:33.720
Yeah, we could take them out of the sticks

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.340
and do them old carrot crunches up.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:37.600
Shall we go for Look 37 then?

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:40.900
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:42:41.990 --> 00:42:45.010
You bought this, son.

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:47.010
Yeah.

00:42:49.420 --> 00:42:51.240
You know we'd want at every auction.

00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:51.920
Linda, Dale.

00:42:52.360 --> 00:42:54.740
Yeah, get two for the same price at this one.

00:42:55.300 --> 00:42:56.060
There we go.

00:42:56.620 --> 00:42:58.380
This stuff is a load of rubbish.

00:42:58.480 --> 00:42:59.980
I know, I did try to warn you.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:00.440
Rodney's?

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.120
Yeah, but I thought...

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:05.600
Yeah, no, the trouble with you is, Rodney, that you will insist on thinking.

00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:08.350
Well, what have you bought, then?

00:43:09.050 --> 00:43:11.070
I got those crystal goblets that you were after.

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:14.640
What are these things?

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:15.340
What, those?

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:17.560
They are lawnmower engines.

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:19.090
Lawnmower engines?

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:22.590
Listen, they're not ordinary lawnmower engines.

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:26.540
No?

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:29.980
They're broken...

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:30.540
normal.

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:46.040
Thel, we'll probably have a few problems getting these back to the depot.

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:48.720
Yeah, we come down on the green line, Sue.

00:43:50.170 --> 00:43:53.370
Oh, well, your best bet is to hire an open-back truck then, innit?

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:59.690
Yeah, but we was wondering whether you could take maybe a few in the back of the van.

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:02.270
Back of my van?

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:06.220
You must be joking.

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:09.140
I've only just cleared them out of the van.

00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:15.690
You mean you were selling them in the first place?

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:16.130
Yeah.

00:44:17.620 --> 00:44:19.780
That is the rubbish that Alfie Flowers sold me.

00:44:20.810 --> 00:44:22.450
Normally I'd never have bought it, but, you know,

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:25.590
he caught me when I was a bit non-competentist down the 111 club.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:29.580
Well, I never thought I'd ever get shot of them.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:31.880
You know me, Rodney. He who dares, wins.

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:35.570
Actually made a tidy little profit on it and all.

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:40.960
What are you supposed to do with them?

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:42.670
Well, why don't you do what I did?

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:46.120
Find yourself a couple of right little plonkers with cash on the itch.

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:54.200
What are we going to do?

00:44:56.770 --> 00:44:58.820
Wait till he ain't looking and run away.

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:02.510
No, we can't do that. He's got my address.

00:45:03.160 --> 00:45:04.380
Yeah, well, he ain't got mine.

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:06.420
Oh, thanks, partner.

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.960
Well, you would insist on bidding for them.

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.360
Yeah, and who wanted to go down to the sticks

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:13.300
and flog them to the carrot crunchers?

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:16.220
Well, you said they were combine harvesters and tractors.

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:17.320
The way you were talking,

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:19.120
we were going to do a deal with Wheatabits.

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.080
Oh, get off my face.

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:23.910
We're going to get home there, anyway.

00:45:26.210 --> 00:45:26.770
Hello, Grandad.

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:28.150
Look at this.

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:29.970
Brought you some strawberries. Go on, dip in.

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:33.850
Oh, dear. They ain't very big, are they?

00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:35.380
What do you mean, they ain't very big?

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:37.280
You wouldn't like one of those up your nose for a walk, would you?

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:43.700
Shut up and eat up. I've got the kettle on.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:46.360
You're splashing out a bit, aren't you?

00:45:46.950 --> 00:45:49.650
Yeah, well, I've had a right blinding week I've sold the loft.

00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:53.190
Here, you even sold those technicoloured woolen teacosies I bought.

00:45:53.720 --> 00:45:54.760
How do you manage that?

00:45:54.940 --> 00:45:57.440
Who the hell wants woolen teacosies these days?

00:45:57.800 --> 00:45:59.860
No, no, no, no, look. I've got that Mrs Murphy.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.920
to stit up all the holes and then I whipped down to the youth centre

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.700
and I flogged them to the West Indian lads, a soppy hat.

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:11.060
There you go.

00:46:11.380 --> 00:46:13.040
Look, there's the housekeeping money, all right.

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:14.720
And look at that, there's a tenner for yourself.

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:16.010
Oh, cheers, dear.

00:46:16.250 --> 00:46:17.310
That's it. Don't squander it.

00:46:17.490 --> 00:46:19.870
No, no, I'll invest it wisely.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:24.200
How's young Rodney doing?

00:46:24.620 --> 00:46:26.940
Ah, well, the opposition are floundering somewhat.

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:29.980
Well, to be more precise, they're going down...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.340
like a one-legged man doing the okey-cokey.

00:46:34.420 --> 00:46:38.000
I've seen Rodney skulking around the garden centres and what have you.

00:46:38.320 --> 00:46:40.620
He ain't got rid of them door-master engines yet.

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:41.140
No.

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:43.430
They're still in their depot.

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:46.350
Well, depot, that's Mickey Pearce's garden shed.

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:48.300
Yeah, they're talking about that.

00:46:49.700 --> 00:46:51.360
Do you know what happened last Tuesday night?

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:53.960
Somebody broke into their shed and nicked two of them engines.

00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:56.120
Oh, no, that's rotten, isn't it?

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:58.410
I feel sorry for young Rodney.

00:46:58.650 --> 00:46:59.970
No, no, no, it's all right.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.260
because Wednesday night they broke in again and put them back.

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:04.920
Here he is.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:08.560
Listen, Del Boy, don't say nothing about them lawnmower engines.

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:10.980
I think he's getting a bit embarrassed about it.

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:12.400
I won't mention them.

00:47:17.240 --> 00:47:17.620
All right, Del?

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:19.340
Triff, Brill Rodders.

00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:20.620
Had a blinding week. How about you?

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:22.700
Oh, fine. Could not be better.

00:47:26.620 --> 00:47:27.920
Sold those lawnmower engines yet?

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:34.760
Oh, no, no. We've had lots of inquiries, obviously,

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:36.740
but we're hanging on for the right price, you know.

00:47:36.960 --> 00:47:38.120
Oh, that is the way, Rodney.

00:47:39.580 --> 00:47:41.120
Agent provocateur, as the French would say.

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:42.160
Well, that's what I thought.

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:47.120
Oh, that reminds me. Did the paper boy bring my son this morning?

00:47:47.550 --> 00:47:49.590
Well, we've had to cancel it, Rodney.

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:50.800
Cancel it? Why?

00:47:51.020 --> 00:47:51.980
Well, you haven't paid your bill, have you?

00:47:52.560 --> 00:47:54.420
What, I'm paying that separate as well now, am I?

00:47:54.540 --> 00:47:56.100
Yeah, well, you're on your own now, remember?

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:59.430
Oh, yeah, yeah, it's all right. I'm just saying, you know, as long as I know.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:01.040
I'll go and pay it tomorrow.

00:48:01.610 --> 00:48:02.890
You hungry, Rodney?

00:48:04.280 --> 00:48:08.300
Uh, well, I had a pretty hefty lunch with a client earlier on,

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:10.840
but, um, yeah, I reckon I could manage some egg and chips.

00:48:11.020 --> 00:48:12.620
I'll go and put the pan off.

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:14.080
Just a minute, just a minute.

00:48:14.260 --> 00:48:15.820
Has he paid his housekeeping money?

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:19.980
Um, well, I've got a bit of a cash flow problem at the moment.

00:48:20.890 --> 00:48:22.550
Well, so's half the people on this estate,

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:24.590
but they don't come in here eating my egg and chips.

00:48:26.060 --> 00:48:28.020
That's all right. I'll pay double next week.

00:48:28.220 --> 00:48:29.420
Ah, well, that's all right, then.

00:48:30.570 --> 00:48:32.110
You can have double egg and chips next week.

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:36.460
Cash flow problem, Rodney.

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:39.980
I thought you had nearly £200 left out of your share.

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:43.420
Yeah, yeah, that's right, but Nicky's holding the money.

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:49.620
Well, he's financial director, see.

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:52.340
Why don't you pop round his house and get some money?

00:48:52.730 --> 00:48:56.330
Yeah, yeah, I would, but he's out of town at the moment.

00:48:56.610 --> 00:48:59.910
Yeah, I thought I hadn't seen him around for about four or five days.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:07.030
no no well that's cause um we're doing this really big deal you see and Mickey's gonna wait

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:13.350
to tie up all the loose ends oh well that explains it yeah explains what no I just thought I saw his

00:49:13.350 --> 00:49:19.400
mum this morning she said she just got a postcard from him from Benidorm he's doing all right you

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:25.670
know the weather's fine food's good met this Swedish bird called Helga oh would that be the

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:29.820
contact that he went to meet? What? Yeah, yeah.

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:38.000
yeah well i've got to admire your bottle rodders i must admit you've been in the business five

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:46.220
minutes and already you've opened up a spanish branch cornered the world market on broken lawn

00:49:46.220 --> 00:49:53.790
mower engines what's your partner doing now is he buying second-hand pedlows no no no nothing like

00:49:53.790 --> 00:49:59.300
that no we're um we're going into the self-catering holiday trade oh what on 200 nicker

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:01.700
Yeah, well, we're starting in a small way.

00:50:02.380 --> 00:50:03.760
Well, you've got a windy house.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:15.400
Prepare to discuss the situation any longer, all right?

00:50:15.620 --> 00:50:17.080
It's confidential information.

00:50:17.420 --> 00:50:19.930
It's right. I understand, Rodney.

00:50:20.110 --> 00:50:21.820
No, no, I understand.

00:50:24.030 --> 00:50:25.030
Well, I'm off out.

00:50:25.650 --> 00:50:26.170
Where are you going?

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:29.870
Well, I thought I might go down and have a couple of flight hours down to Nag's Head and ink.

00:50:30.250 --> 00:50:32.890
go on to the Star of Bengal for a Ruby Murray.

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:35.180
Coming?

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:37.000
What?

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:43.280
Er, no. No, I really ought to stay in and do the company accounts, I suppose.

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:44.800
Oh, here they are.

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:45.400
Oh, cheers.

00:51:03.360 --> 00:51:05.920
You don't really think I'm that hard, do you, Rodney?

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:10.750
Of course I'm not.

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:12.330
Oh, cheers, Dale.

00:51:14.350 --> 00:51:16.050
Grandad, do him their Megan chips, will you?

